Yes, you **can** create a hover effect that shows a visual preview of an element from a different page—but it’s not straightforward and comes with important limitations. Here’s how you can approach it, along with realistic options:

---

### 🔍 **Why It’s Challenging**
Web browsers **don’t allow direct access** to content from another page (for security reasons). You can’t just "peek" at another page’s HTML or DOM on hover.

So you **can’t** directly fetch and show a preview of an element from a different page via client-side JavaScript alone—unless that content is **accessible via an API or shared resource**.

---

### ✅ **Practical Solutions**

Here are several ways to achieve a **hover preview of an element on another page**, depending on your needs:

---

### ✅ Option 1: **Preload the Preview as an Image (Simplest)**
If you control both pages, you can **pre-generate a static image** of the target element and use it as a preview on hover.

#### How:
- Use a tool (like **Puppeteer**, **Playwright**, or **Screenshot API**) to take a screenshot of the other page/element when it’s loaded.
- Save the image (e.g., `preview-of-page2-element.jpg`).
- On your current page, use CSS `:hover` + `img` or `background-image` to show the preview.

#### Example:
```html
<!-- On your first page -->
<div class="hover-preview-container">
  <a href="/page2">Go to Page 2</a>
  <div class="preview">
    <img src="images/preview-of-page2-element.jpg" alt="Preview of element on Page 2">
  </div>
</div>
```

```css
.hover-preview-container {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
}

.preview {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  left: 0;
  width: 300px;
  height: 200px;
  background: white;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  overflow: hidden;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.3s ease;
  z-index: 10;
}

.hover-preview-container:hover .preview {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

> ✅ Fast, simple, safe.  
> ❌ Requires manual/automated image updates if content changes.

---

### ✅ Option 2: **Load Preview via API (Dynamic)**
If the element on the other page is data-driven (e.g. a product card), expose that data via an **API**.

#### Example:
- Let’s say you want to preview the “Featured Product” from `/page2`.
- On `/page2`, have a JSON endpoint like: `https://yoursite.com/api/product/featured`.

#### On your current page:
```html
<div class="hover-preview" data-preview-url="/api/product/featured">
  Hover to preview
  <div class="preview-modal"></div>
</div>
```

```javascript
document.querySelectorAll('.hover-preview').forEach(el => {
  el.addEventListener('mouseenter', async () => {
    const url = el.dataset.previewUrl;
    const response = await fetch(url);
    const data = await response.json();

    const modal = el.querySelector('.preview-modal');
    modal.innerHTML = `
      <h4>${data.title}</h4>
      <img src="${data.image}" alt="${data.title}" />
      <p>${data.description}</p>
    `;
    modal.style.display = 'block';
  });

  el.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
    el.querySelector('.preview-modal').style.display = 'none';
  });
});
```

> ✅ Highly dynamic and real-time.  
> ❌ Requires backend API support on the other page.

---

### ✅ Option 3: **Use an Iframe (Limited)**
You *can* embed another page in an iframe and reveal it on hover—**but only if the other page allows it** (`X-Frame-Options` and `Content-Security-Policy` must not block embeds).

#### Example:
```html
<div class="hover-iframe-preview">
  <a href="/page2">Hover over me</a>
  <iframe src="/page2#element-id" class="preview-frame" sandbox="allow-scripts"></iframe>
</div>
```

```css
.hover-iframe-preview {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
}

.preview-frame {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  left: 0;
  width: 400px;
  height: 300px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.3s;
  z-index: 100;
  display: none;
}

.hover-iframe-preview:hover .preview-frame {
  opacity: 1;
  display: block;
}
```

> ⚠️ **Limitation:** The target page must not block framing (`X-Frame-Options: DENY` or `SAMEORIGIN` will prevent it).
> ❌ You can't preview **just an element**; you show the whole page fragment.

---

### ✅ Option 4: **Shared Component via JS (Recommended for SPAs)**
If your website uses a framework like React, Vue, or Svelte, and both pages share components, you can **re-use the component**.

#### Example (React):
```jsx
// Reusable component
function FeaturedProductCard({ product }) {
  return (
    <div className="preview-card">
      <img src={product.image} alt={product.title} />
      <h4>{product.title}</h4>
    </div>
  );
}

// On your first page:
function HomePage() {
  return (
    <div className="hover-preview">
      <a href="/page2">Preview Page 2's Product</a>
      <HoverPreview>
        <FeaturedProductCard product={mockProductData} />
      </HoverPreview>
    </div>
  );
}
```

> ✅ Perfect if you’re building a single-page app (SPA).  
> ✅ No cross-page issues; same code reused.

---

### 🎯 **Summary: Which Should You Choose?**

| Method                          | Dynamic? | Easy? | Secure? | Best For |
|----------------------------------|----------|-------|---------|----------|
| Static image preview             | ❌       | ✅✅    | ✅       | Simple, fast previews |
| API-driven data preview          | ✅       | ✅     | ✅       | Dynamic content (products, cards) |
| Iframe preview                   | ✅       | ⚠️     | ❌ (if blocked) | When you can't control content |
| Shared UI component (SPA)        | ✅       | ✅✅   | ✅       | Modern web apps with shared code |

---

### ✅ Final Recommendation:
Use **Option 1 (static image)** for simplicity, or **Option 2 (API)** if you want it dynamic and scalable.

